{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Accelerating Frontier Science with a Living, Probabilistic Evidence Graph\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Authors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi0 Joel Dietz\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Created\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi0 June 19, 2026\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abstract\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rate-limiting step in frontier science is increasingly not generating data but {\i synthesizing} it: reading a flood of papers, weighing how strongly the evidence supports each hypothesis, and deciding what to test next. We describe Probknow, a system and methodology that accelerates this loop by maintaining a {\i living, probabilistic evidence graph} of a field. Nodes are hypotheses; edges are evidential and citation relations; node confidence is an explicit, aggregate {\i weight of evidence} (in decibans, derived from per-result Bayes factors via Vovk\u8211-Sellke calibration); and the whole graph is versioned through time so hypotheses can be watched as they strengthen or weaken\u8212-turning \u8220"where is this field heading, and what should we measure next?\u8221" into a query rather than a literature review. Under the hood, each claim is stored as a machine-readable, independently verifiable unit (a nanopublication) carrying its assertion, context, evidence links, and an agent-attributed Bayesian assessment; this substrate is what makes confidence computable, auditable, and reusable, but it is a means, not the end. In deployment the graph spans 4,886 papers, 48,561 extracted claims, and 1,299 hypotheses across 88 domains, with hypotheses optionally carrying a dated economic forecast and a cross-model probability panel. We ground the system in a bioelectricity case study\u8212-a corpus of eighteen \u8220"wave\u8221" claims from the Levin-lab regeneration and pattern-formation literature\u8212-and show the loop closing onto an active wet lab: calcium-imaging measurements of injured {\i Xenopus} embryo pairs are quantified, calibrated, and folded back in as new evidence, while the graph\u8217's confidence gaps nominate the next bench experiment. We discuss how this accelerates discovery (front-and-center uncertainty, machine-actionable synthesis, experiment prioritization), its limitations (model-assisted assessment, independence assumptions, calibration as a bound), and the provenance practices required when large language models assist in evidence appraisal.\par}
