{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Table 1. Frontier-science landscape in the deployed Probknow graph, grouped by theme (71 domains; a separate \u8220"contested claims\u8221" vertical of 17 domains and 122 hypotheses is tracked apart so the two intellectual modes do not mix). Live counts as of June 19, 2026.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b An economic prioritization layer.} Beyond evidential strength, each hypothesis can carry dated economic predictions that move the question from \u8220"what is true?\u8221" toward \u8220"what is worth testing next?\u8221". Across the corpus there are 7,338 such predictions with a sized market, totalling roughly $45.8 trillion in addressable value at an average of 6.0 years to achievement. This gives the experiment-prioritization signal of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#the-living-evidence-graph"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 7
}}}
 a second axis\u8212-expected economic impact\u8212-orthogonal to the evidence weight, so a lab can rank candidate experiments by both how much {\i belief} and how much {\i value} a result would move. (These forecasts are model-generated and should be read as calibrated opinion, not investment advice.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The Structured Probabilistic Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The atomic unit is a {\i structured probabilistic claim} (SPC), declared to conform to the schema {\f1 spc/1.0}. An SPC is more than a sentence: it is an assertion plus everything needed to weigh and re-use it. Its fields are:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Assertion} \u8212- a precise, falsifiable statement of the claim ({\f1 dcterms:description}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Context} \u8212- the experimental system, model organism, intervention, and readout that scope the claim ({\f1 ex:context}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Evidence items} \u8212- one or more discrete pieces of support ({\f1 ex:hasEvidence}), each linkable to the literature with the Citation Typing Ontology [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-14"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
14
}}}
] via {\f1 cito:citesAsEvidence}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Assessment} \u8212- a Bayesian weight-of-evidence appraisal ({\f1 ex:hasAssessment}) carrying a combined Bayes factor, a weight of evidence in decibans, the calibration method, and\u8212-crucially\u8212-the {\i agent} that produced it ({\f1 prov:wasAttributedTo}).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This separation lets a downstream consumer accept the assertion, inspect the context, follow the evidence to its sources, and read the strength of support as a single calibrated number, all without parsing prose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bayesian Weight of Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We score each claim by how strongly the reported results favor the working hypothesis {\i H}{\sub 1} over a precise null {\i H}{\sub 0}. For a single result the Bayes factor is\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 $$K = \\frac\{P(\\text\{data\} \\mid H_\{1\})\}\{P(\\text\{data\} \\mid H_\{0\})\},$$\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 and the {\i weight of evidence} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-3"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
3
}}}
, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-4"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
4
}}}
] in decibans is\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\i W}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?10\u8198 ?log{\sub 10}\u8198 ?{\i K}\u8202 ?\u8193 ?[deciban].\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Primary papers usually report a {\i p}-value rather than {\i K}. The Vovk\u8211-Sellke calibration [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-15"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
15
}}}
] provides a defensible upper bound on the Bayes factor that a precise-null {\i p}-value can support,\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 $$K(p) = \\frac\{1\}\{- e\\, p\\,\\ln\\, p\},\\qquad p < e^\{- 1\},$$\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 and {\i K}({\i p}) = 1 otherwise. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-vovk-sellke"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Equation (3)
}}}
 is used conservatively: where a paper reports only a threshold (e.g. {\i p} < 0.01) we take the bound at the threshold, which {\i under}-states the evidence rather than overstating it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Assuming approximate independence across {\i n} evidence items within a claim, Bayes factors multiply and weights of evidence add:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 $$K_\{\\text\{combined\}\} = \\prod\\limits_\{i = 1\}^\{n\}K_\{i\},\\qquad W_\{\\text\{combined\}\} = \\sum\\limits_\{i = 1\}^\{n\}W_\{i\} = 10\\,\\log_\{10\}\\, K_\{\\text\{combined\}\}.$$\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The independence assumption is recorded in the assessment description so that a reader can judge it; correlated evidence inflates {\i K}{\sub combined}, so the number is best read as an optimistic, explicitly-bounded summary rather than a posterior. The additive deciban scale is what the graph layer propagates and what makes confidence comparable across claims and fields.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 An independent model panel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The literature-derived weight of evidence above answers \u8220"how strong are the {\i reported} statistics?\u8221". In deployment we complement it with an orthogonal estimate: a panel of up to six frontier language models (Claude Opus 4.6, GPT-5, OpenAI o3, Gemini 2.5 Pro, DeepSeek, and Grok 4.3) each independently assigns a probability that a given claim is true. Where the models agree, confidence is high; where they diverge, the spread is itself the signal and a built-in calibration check. To date 121 hypotheses carry such a panel, with 966 individual assessments. The per-model mean probabilities ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-model-panel"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 2
}}}
) expose systematic scorer bias\u8212-Gemini is consistently the most generous and Grok the most skeptical\u8212-so the gap between them bounds a model-disagreement uncertainty that any single scorer would hide. The panel does not replace the Vovk\u8211-Sellke weight of evidence; it cross-checks it, and a disagreement between a strong reported statistic and a skeptical panel is exactly the kind of claim a curator should examine first.\par}
{
