{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 A Verifiable Substrate for Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the graph\u8217's confidence values to be trustworthy and reusable, each claim must be stored as an auditable, tamper-evident unit rather than re-extracted prose. We use nanopublications as that substrate. Each SPC is serialized as a nanopublication in RDF/TriG with the canonical four named graphs: {\f1 Head}, {\f1 assertion}, {\f1 provenance}, and {\f1 pubinfo} ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#listing-wave-b"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Listing 1
}}}
). The assertion graph holds the claim and its evidence links; the provenance graph records that the assertion was derived from the cited source ({\f1 prov:wasDerivedFrom}); and the publication-information graph carries the bibliographic citation, timestamps, license, the generating software agent, and the Bayesian assessment with its attributed agent. Identifiers live under a stable {\f1 w3id.org} namespace so they resolve persistently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 ex:claim-AML2007-C1 a ex:Assertion ;\line
  dcterms:description "V-ATPase-driven H+ flux is necessary for tail\line
      regeneration, and a concanamycin-insensitive H+ pump (PMA) is\line
      sufficient to rescue regeneration under V-ATPase inhibition." ;\line
  cito:citesAsEvidence <https://doi.org/10.1242/dev.02812> ;\line
  ex:context "Xenopus laevis tadpole; amputation at stage 41; V-ATPase\line
      inhibition; PMA H+ pump rescue; regeneration index at 7 dpa." ;\line
  ex:hasAssessment ex:assessment-AML2007-C1 ;\line
  ex:hasEvidence ex:evidence-AML2007-C1-E1 .\line
\line
# ... publication-information graph ...\line
ex:assessment-AML2007-C1 a ex:BayesianAssessment ;\line
  ex:bayesFactorCombined 100588.890510 ;\line
  ex:weightOfEvidence_deciban 50.025500 ;\line
  ex:calibrationMethod "Vovk-Sellke p-value calibration" ;\line
  prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/levin-kg/agent/gpt-5-pro> .\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Listing 1. A bioelectricity claim as a nanopublication (excerpt; namespaces and one of four evidence items shown). The assertion is machine-readable, the evidence is a resolvable DOI, and the publication-information graph carries a Bayesian assessment with explicit agent provenance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Canonicalization, trusty URIs, signing, registries.} Before publication a claim is canonicalized (normalizing blank nodes and graph IRIs so logically identical content yields identical bytes), then assigned a {\i trusty URI} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-10"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
10
}}}
] whose suffix is a hash of that content. The nanopublication is signed with an author key bound to an ORCID, and published to a decentralized registry that replicates it [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-11"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
11
}}}
]. The result is a permanent, verifiable, independently-citable claim: anyone can re-fetch it, re-hash it, and confirm both its integrity and its authorship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The Living Evidence Graph\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Published claims are ingested into Probknow, a graph whose semantics are summarized in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-graph-mapping"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 3
}}}
. A node is a hypothesis or claim; an edge is an evidential, citation, or logical relation (supports, refutes, refines, depends-on); node {\i confidence} is an aggregate weight of evidence rolled up from the SPC assessments that bear on it; and every ingest is timestamped so the graph is a versioned object. Because confidence is carried on the additive deciban scale ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-combined"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. 4
}}}
), aggregation across claims and fields is a well-defined sum, and a hypothesis can be {\i watched over time}: as new nanopublications arrive, its confidence rises or falls, and the trajectory itself becomes data.\par}
{
