{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Visually (as on the public Probknow map), node color encodes confidence and node size encodes evidence weight, and a timeline control replays how the graph for a field evolved. This turns \u8220"where is this field heading?\u8221" into a query rather than a literature review.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Case Study: Bioelectricity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We exercise the system on bioelectricity, the study of endogenous voltage and ion-flux signals that instruct growth and form [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-12"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
12
}}}
, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-13"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
13
}}}
]. This is the most developed region of the deployed graph: the bioelectricity-and-regeneration theme spans 486 papers, 119 hypotheses, and 5,745 claims, and the Levin-lab subdomain alone (labeled L1\u8211-L9) accounts for 457 papers, 112 hypotheses, and 5,513 claims\u8212-making it the single most-developed domain in the corpus. It also anchors the economic layer: the largest single predictions attached to any hypothesis sit here, including an AI/device combination to reverse aging (\u8764 ?$1 trillion) and pre-neural bioelectric platforms for whole-organ biofabrication (\u8764 ?$120 billion). Within this subgraph we built a focused set of eighteen \u8220"wave\u8221" nanopublications (labeled A through R), each capturing one or more claims about ion-flux control of regeneration and patterning, with evidence linked to the primary papers by DOI and a Bayesian weight of evidence attached\u8212-the fully-signed, verifiable core of the larger Levin-lab region.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#listing-wave-b"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Listing 1
}}}
 shows wave B: the claim that V-ATPase\u8211-driven H{\super +} flux is necessary, and a concanamycin-insensitive H{\super +} pump sufficient, for {\i Xenopus} tail regeneration [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-1"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
1
}}}
]. Four evidence items from the source were each calibrated through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-vovk-sellke"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. (3)
}}}
 and combined under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-combined"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. (4)
}}}
, yielding a combined Bayes factor of {\i K}{\sub combined} \u8776 ? 1.0 \u215 ? 10{\super 5} and a weight of evidence of {\i W} \u8776 ? 50.0 deciban\u8212-\u8220"decisive\u8221" on Good\u8217's scale\u8212-recorded together with the independence caveat and the assessing agent. One can verify the internal consistency directly: 10\u160 ?log{\sub 10}(100588.89) = 50.03 deciban, matching the stored value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Closing the loop with the wet lab\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The graph is not only fed by the {\i published} literature; in this project it is also fed by, and feeds back into, an active wet lab. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#fig-bench-graph-loop"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Figure 2
}}}
 shows the cycle. At the bench, pairs of {\i Xenopus laevis} embryos expressing a calcium reporter are imaged by time-lapse fluorescence microscopy while one embryo (\u8220"A\u8221") receives a controlled mechanical injury (a \u8220"poke\u8221"), in both direct-contact and no-contact configurations and in different head/tail orientations. The microscope emits multi-page TIFF stacks, which a quantification pipeline (the {\f1 wave-vector-analysis} tooling in the project repository) turns into numbers: it detects bright calcium \u8220"sparks\u8221" frame by frame, tracks them, segments the two embryos, assigns head/tail and anterior\u8211-posterior coordinates, maps events onto a normalized region atlas, and rolls per-frame detections up into per-cluster wave vectors ({\f1 spark_tracks.csv} \u8594 ? {\f1 vector_clusters.csv}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1800\pich827\picwgoal8668\pichgoal3982 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fig. 2. The closed bench\u8596 ?graph loop in the bioelectricity case study. Calcium imaging of injured {\i Xenopus} embryo pairs is quantified by the project\u8217's wave-vector pipeline into measured wave properties; these become calibrated evidence nodes published as nanopublications and ingested into Probknow; and the graph\u8217's confidence gaps in turn nominate the next experiment. Green = wet lab, blue = instrument/compute, orange = knowledge graph.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These measurements are exactly the {\i evidence items} the SPC schema expects. A wet-lab observation such as \u8220"injuring embryo A raises calcium activity in both embryos\u8221" becomes a claim whose evidence is a measured post-injury activity ratio; \u8220"the response in B is a directional wave\u8221" becomes a claim backed by measured wave directions (mean \u8776 ? 5\u8211-21\u176 ?) and speeds (\u8776 ? 3\u8211-4 px s{\super \u8722 ?1}); \u8220"the local response co-locates with the wound\u8221" becomes a claim backed by a measured distance-from-poke distribution. Each such observation is calibrated ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#bayesian-weight-of-evidence"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5
}}}
), serialized as a nanopublication ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#a-verifiable-substrate-for-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 6
}}}
), and ingested into Probknow as a {\i new evidence node} attached to the corresponding literature hypothesis\u8212-the dashed re-weighting arrow of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#fig-probknow-pipeline"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Figure 1
}}}
, now realized with primary data rather than only re-read papers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, the graph also points back {\i toward} the bench. Because every hypothesis carries an explicit weight of evidence and an explicit set of evidence items, the graph makes its own gaps legible: some hypotheses are already well-supported, some are only {\i partially} testable with the data in hand, and some are {\i not yet testable} at all because they require conditions or metadata the current assay does not capture (for example, a graded distance series, embryo-age staging, or a contraction readout). That triage\u8212-which under-supported or untestable node would move the most evidence weight if measured next\u8212-is precisely an experiment-prioritization signal. The loop is therefore closed in both directions: bench measurements raise or lower confidence in graph hypotheses, and the graph\u8217's confidence gaps nominate the next bench experiment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How this accelerates discovery.} The payoff is a faster discovery loop. Synthesis that previously required reading and mentally reconciling a field becomes a query over a graph whose node confidences are explicit and comparable; uncertainty is front-and-center rather than buried in a results section; and because the graph is time-versioned, the {\i dynamics} of belief are observable rather than reconstructed after the fact. Most directly, the graph\u8217's confidence gaps act as an experiment-prioritization signal, shortening the path from \u8220"what is known\u8221" to \u8220"what to measure next.\u8221" That claims also become verifiable, citable, machine-readable objects is what makes this computable\u8212-but it is the means, not the goal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Model-assisted appraisal and provenance.} In our corpus the Bayesian assessments were produced with the assistance of a large language model, recorded explicitly as a software agent via {\f1 prov:wasAttributedTo}. We regard transparent agent attribution as a requirement, not an option: a reader must be able to see that an appraisal was model-assisted, which model, and under what stated assumptions, so that it can be audited or redone. The nanopublication substrate makes this provenance non-optional and immutable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Limitations.} (1) The Vovk\u8211-Sellke value is a {\i bound}, not a posterior; reported strengths are optimistic by construction and should be read as upper limits. (2) The independence assumption in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-combined"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. (4)
}}}
 is frequently violated by multiple readouts of one experiment; we mitigate by recording it, but correlated evidence inflates the combined factor. (3) Claim extraction and calibration are only as good as the (possibly model-assisted) reader, motivating the strict provenance above. (4) Registries deliberately do not support bulk upload, so publishing at scale requires either rate-limited batching or operating one\u8217's own registry node.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Effect size, not just significance.} A weight of evidence derived from a {\i p}-value answers \u8220"how surprising are these data under the null?\u8221" but is silent on \u8220"how large is the effect?\u8221"\u8212-and at the small sample sizes typical of frontier experiments the two can diverge sharply. We therefore pair each calibrated {\i p}-value with a nonparametric effect-size estimate (Cliff\u8217's {\i \u948 ?} for two-group contrasts, the matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation for paired designs), so that a claim node carries both a strength-of-evidence and a magnitude. This is more than bookkeeping: the combination defines a useful triage state for the wet lab. A {\i large effect that is not yet significant}\u8212-a real-looking signal that is simply underpowered\u8212-is exactly the confidence gap the graph should flag as a high-value next experiment, whereas a {\i significant but negligible} effect (common with large {\i n}) warns against over-promoting a claim. In the bioelectricity case study these effect sizes are computed with nonparametric, paired, and batch-aware tests appropriate to small, non-normal, clutch-clustered calcium-imaging data.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Future work.} Principled handling of dependent evidence; community curation and counter-nanopublications that {\i refute} or {\i refine} existing claims; calibration against held-out replication outcomes; carrying effect-size magnitude alongside the weight of evidence as a second confidence coordinate that drives experiment prioritization; and richer edge semantics for mechanistic chains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We described Probknow, a system and methodology for {\i accelerating frontier science} by maintaining a living, probabilistic evidence graph that synthesizes a field and prioritizes its next experiments. Confidence is an explicit, additive Bayesian weight of evidence; the graph is versioned so that belief can be watched as it evolves; and a verifiable nanopublication substrate\u8212-the enabling machinery, not the headline\u8212-makes that confidence computable, auditable, and reusable. A bioelectricity case study shows the loop closing onto an active wet lab, from primary measurement to calibrated evidence node to a prioritized next experiment. The aim is a literature one does not only read, but can query, weigh, watch\u8212-and act on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Reproducibility.} The nanopublication corpus, the SPC schema, and the publishing/canonicalization tooling used here are available in the project repository; each published claim is independently verifiable through its trusty URI.\par}
